var _markdown = "\n\nData hooks are the client-side face of the data plane. They expose the full query/mutation feature set — caching, invalidation, optimistic updates, prefetch, infinite lists, suspense — with keys derived automatically from **model and controller identity**. You never hand-build a query key, and you never duplicate a loader's fetch: hooks read the same cache loaders already populated.\n\nThe hook engine is a framework-owned query engine (ADR-0016): the full query feature surface is passed through, but the engine itself is internal and never imported by application code. What you interact with is the `@kwiva/react` hook surface.\n\n## The Hook Model [#the-hook-model]\n\nEvery hook is keyed by its model (or controller) plus its arguments. `useList(Post, { where: ... })` builds a cache entry whose identity is derived from the `Post` model and that exact query — so `invalidate(Post)` knows precisely which entries to clear. Loader-dehydrated results use identical keys, which is why entering a page from SSR never refetches what the loader already delivered:\n\n```plaintext title=\"the-hook-model.txt\"\nloader (server)  → dehydrated into stream     → client cache key = Post + query\nuseResource/useList (client) → same key       → reads the entry, never refetches\n```\n\n## Hook Reference [#hook-reference]\n\n| Hook              | Purpose                                      | Typical signature                                     |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `useResource`     | Single record by id                          | `useResource(Post, id)`                               |\n| `useList`         | Query a list with where/order/pagination     | `useList(Post, { where, orderBy, page })`             |\n| `useMutation`     | Create/update/delete with optimistic support | `useMutation(Post.update, { optimistic, onSuccess })` |\n| `useInfiniteList` | Infinite, cursor-based lists                 | `useInfiniteList(Post, { cursor, limit })`            |\n| `useApi`          | Escape hatch for ad-hoc keyed queries        | `useApi([key], fetcher)`                              |\n\n## useResource [#useresource]\n\n```tsx title=\"useresource.tsx\"\nimport { useResource } from '@kwiva/react'\n\nconst { data: post, isPending, error } = useResource(Post, id)\n```\n\nReads a single record by model identity and id. The same cache entry the detail loader dehydrated into — navigating from a list to a detail page shows instant data when the record is already present. Returns the standard query result shape: `data`, `isPending`, `isFetching`, `error`, and the rest of the pass-through option surface.\n\n## useList [#uselist]\n\n```tsx title=\"uselist.tsx\"\nconst { data, isFetching } = useList(Post, {\n  where: { status: 'published' },\n  orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' },\n  staleTime: 30_000,\n})\n```\n\nList queries pass through the full option surface, including `staleTime`, `gcTime`, `refetchOnWindowFocus`, `refetchInterval`, `select`, `placeholderData`, and `enabled`. The `where` clause is typed against the model IR — a filter on a field the model does not declare is a type error, and pagination keys compose with `invalidate(Post)` scoping.\n\n## useMutation [#usemutation]\n\nMutations are model-scoped and automatically invalidate what they touch:\n\n```tsx title=\"usemutation.tsx\"\nimport { useMutation, invalidate } from '@kwiva/react'\n\nconst save = useMutation(Post.update, {\n  optimistic: (input, current) => ({ ...current, ...input }),\n  onSuccess: () => invalidate(Post),\n})\n\nawait save.mutate({ title: 'New title' })\n```\n\nThe `optimistic` helper applies the input to the current cache entry immediately, giving an instant UI response; the call itself goes through the typed RPC client; on success, model-scoped invalidation refreshes every list and detail that depends on the model:\n\n```tsx title=\"usemutation-2.tsx\"\nconst { mutate, isMutating } = useMutation(Post.create, { input })\n```\n\nMutations accept the model methods — `Post.create`, `Post.update`, `Post.delete` — and custom controller actions, typed from the same route manifest the client is. Errors map through the typed error contract, so an `errorComponent` or a form can render per-field issues from the mutation's result. See [RPC Client](/docs/frontend/rpc-client).\n\n## useInfiniteList [#useinfinitelist]\n\n```tsx title=\"useinfinitelist.tsx\"\nconst feed = useInfiniteList(Post, { cursor: 'createdAt', limit: 20 })\n```\n\nInfinite lists page by cursor. Keep loading the next page as the user scrolls; each page is cached under the same model identity, so revisiting a feed does not reset the scroll position or refetch history. Cursor choice is typed against the model's fields — any sortable field works as the pagination key.\n\n## Prefetching [#prefetching]\n\nWarm the cache ahead of a render from a loader or from code:\n\n```tsx title=\"prefetching.tsx\"\n// inside a loader, via the typed client\nloader: async ({ client }) =>\n  client.prefetch(['reports', 'summary'], () => client.reports.summary())\n```\n\n```tsx title=\"prefetching-2.tsx\"\n// prefetchQuery from application code\nconst { prefetchQuery } = usePrefetch()\nawait prefetchQuery(useList(Post, { page: 1 }))\n```\n\nPrefetched entries share keys with loader and hook results — preloading, navigating, and rendering all hit one cache. The `usePrefetch` surface is also what intent preloading drives under the hood, so `<Link preload=\"intent\">` and explicit prefetches land in the same entries. See [Navigation & Link](/docs/frontend/navigation).\n\n## Cache Control [#cache-control]\n\nCache semantics are passed through per query:\n\n| Option                 | Effect                                                                    |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `staleTime`            | How long a fresh result is considered current before a background refetch |\n| `gcTime`               | How long an unused entry is retained before garbage collection            |\n| `refetchOnWindowFocus` | Refetch inactive data when the tab regains focus                          |\n| `refetchInterval`      | Polling interval for live data                                            |\n| `retry`                | Retry policy for failed queries                                           |\n| `select`               | Derived, memoised projection of the data                                  |\n| `placeholderData`      | Value shown while the query is pending                                    |\n| `suspense`             | Drive rendering from wrapping suspense instead of `isPending`             |\n| `enabled`              | Gate a query on a condition (e.g. a selected id)                          |\n\nThese are the query engine options passed through unchanged — the cache behavior is the full query-engine model, keyed the Kwiva way.\n\n## Invalidation [#invalidation]\n\nInvalidation is **model-scoped** by default. Keys derive from model identity plus arguments, so a single call clears every derived key:\n\n```tsx title=\"invalidation.tsx\"\ninvalidate(Post)\n```\n\nclears every list, detail, and custom entry touching the `Post` model. On the server side, `invalidateTags` clears route-rule caches (see [Caching Strategies](/docs/rendering/caching)). The two layers are separate: hooks refresh client cache, tags purge server route cache. Model writes also purge the server-side **model cache** automatically, so reads never go stale after a write on the same model.\n\n## Suspense [#suspense]\n\n```tsx title=\"suspense.tsx\"\nconst { data } = useResource(Post, id, { suspense: true })\n```\n\nWith suspense enabled, hooks throw to the nearest boundary while pending — the same boundaries that render streamed `loaderData` segments. Error boundaries and deferred segments share one pattern across loaders and hooks, so a page can mix a blocking loader value, a streamed deferred value, and a suspense-enabled hook under the same boundary tree. See [Streaming SSR](/docs/rendering/streaming).\n\n## Real-Time Subscriptions [#real-time-subscriptions]\n\nLive data flows through the same hook surface. Channel subscriptions consume the typed client's realtime endpoints, and updates key into the shared cache like any query. See [Channels](/docs/realtime/channels) and [Real-Time Data on the Client](/docs/realtime/client-usage).\n\n## The Escape Hatch [#the-escape-hatch]\n\nWhen you need a custom, non-model query — a report, an aggregation, a controller-only endpoint — key it explicitly:\n\n```tsx title=\"the-escape-hatch.tsx\"\nconst report = useApi(['report', id], () => client.reports.get(id))\n```\n\nThe key participates in cache and invalidation like any other: its identity is explicit where model hooks are derived. Use it sparingly — the derived keys of the model hooks are usually sufficient.\n\n## Forms & Mutations [#forms--mutations]\n\nSchema-driven forms are built on the same cache. `useForm(Post.create)` derives fields from the model's validation rules, maps server-side errors per field through the typed client's error contract, and submits through the mutation pipeline — no separate form library required (v1.x). See [Data Validation](/docs/data/validation) for the field rules forms derive from.\n\n## Devtools [#devtools]\n\n`@kwiva/react/devtools` ships a skinned Query devtools panel — inspect cache entries, replay invalidations, and watch query states during development.\n\n## Rules [#rules]\n\n* Components use data hooks over model identity — no hand-built query keys\n* Mutations go through the typed client, with invalidation on success\n* Loaders prefetch into the same cache; clients never double-fetch what a loader supplied\n* No engine imports — the query engine is internal\n\n## What's Next [#whats-next]\n\n* [RPC Client](/docs/frontend/rpc-client) — the typed transport behind the hooks\n* [Loaders & Data](/docs/frontend/loaders) — the server side population of the same cache\n* [Caching Strategies](/docs/rendering/caching) — client cache, route cache, and model cache together\n* [Real-Time Data](/docs/realtime/client-usage) — `useChannel` for channel subscriptions\n* [Data Models](/docs/data/models) — the model identity hooks are keyed by\n";
